senses.gloss:
puryaṣṭaka
(‘city [or body] comprising eight [parts]’)
------------------------------------------
It is the सूक्ष्मशरीर or the subtle body that is responsible for the transmigration of the जीव, the सोउल् in bondage.
According to some schools of शैविस्म् this सूक्ष्मशरीर comprises eight parts and hence is called the ‘puryaṣṭaka’ (purī = the city [of nine gates] or the body; अष्टक = group of eight).
These eight are:
the five तन्मात्रस् (subtle elements);
the fives thūlabhūtas (gross elements)
the five ज्ञानेन्द्रियस् (organs of sense);
the five कर्मेन्द्रियस् (organs of action);
the अन्तःकरण (or the inner organ comprising मनस् or मिन्द्, बुद्धि or the intellect and अहङ्कार or ego-sense);
the three guṇas (sattva, रजस् and तमस्);
the प्रधान or प्रकृति;
the पञ्चतत्त्वस् or the five principles viz., कला (limited powers of action), kāla (time), niyati (law of कर्म), विद्या (limited knowledge) and राग (attachment).
See also ŚAIVISM.
